---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import { href } from '../config'

const sections = [
  ['install', 'Install'], ['signin', 'Sign in to GitHub'], ['vault', 'Create a vault'], ['secret', 'Add a secret and use it'], ['apps', 'Use it in your apps'], ['node', 'Node.js and TypeScript'], ['servers', 'Servers and CI'],
]
---
<Base title="Docs" current="Docs">
  <div class="docs">
    <aside class="side">
      <div><h4>GETTING STARTED</h4>{sections.map(([id, t], i) => <a href={`#${id}`} aria-current={i === 0 ? 'true' : undefined}>{t}</a>)}</div>
      <div><h4>REFERENCE</h4><a href={href('/security/')}>Security model</a><a href={href('/changelog/')}>Changelog</a><a href={href('/download/')}>Download</a></div>
    </aside>
    <article class="article">
      <span class="crumb">Docs / Getting started</span>
      <h1 class="h1" style="font-size:2.75rem">Get started</h1>
      <p class="lead" style="max-width:640px">Install Gitvault, sign in with GitHub and store your first secret. It takes about two minutes, and the desktop app and the CLI use the same vault.</p>

      <h2 id="install"><i>01</i>Install</h2>
      <p>Download the installer for Windows or Ubuntu from the <a class="green" href={href('/download/')}>download page</a>. Gitvault needs git on your PATH.</p>

      <h2 id="signin"><i>02</i>Sign in to GitHub</h2>
      <p>Pick whichever fits. The GitHub CLI route needs no setup if you already use gh.</p>
      <div>
        <div class="opt"><b>GitHub CLI</b><p>Run gh auth login once, then choose "Continue with GitHub CLI" in the app.</p></div>
        <div class="opt"><b>Personal access token</b><p>Create a token with the repo scope and paste it into the app or pipe it to the CLI.</p></div>
        <div class="opt"><b>Device flow</b><p>Sign in from your browser. Needs an OAuth app with Device Flow enabled.</p></div>
      </div>

      <h2 id="vault"><i>03</i>Create a vault</h2>
      <p>A vault is a private GitHub repository holding only encrypted files. Choose a master password and save the recovery key shown once.</p>
      <pre class="code"><span class="g">$</span> gitvault auth login --from-gh
<span class="g">$</span> gitvault vault create --name Personal
<span class="c"># prompts for a master password and prints your recovery key</span></pre>
      <div class="callout"><b>Keep your recovery key safe</b><p>It is the only way back in if you forget your master password. Gitvault cannot recover it for you.</p></div>

      <h2 id="secret"><i>04</i>Add a secret and use it</h2>
      <p>Add secrets in the app, or from the terminal. The value comes from stdin or a hidden prompt, never from an argument.</p>
      <pre class="code"><span class="g">$</span> echo -n "sk_live_…" | gitvault secret add STRIPE_KEY \
    --environment production --value-stdin
<span class="g">$</span> gitvault secret list
<span class="g">$</span> gitvault run --environment production -- ./deploy.sh
<span class="c"># secrets injected as environment variables, never printed</span>
<span class="g">$</span> gitvault sync now</pre>

      <h2 id="apps"><i>05</i>Use it in your apps</h2>
      <p>Anything can run under the CLI: <code>gitvault run</code> injects an environment as variables and starts your command. Node.js apps can also load secrets in code, with no .env file in the project.</p>

      <h3 id="node" class="sub">Node.js and TypeScript</h3>
      <p>The library is written in TypeScript, ships its own types, and works with both <code>import</code> and <code>require</code> on Node 18 or newer. It is published on GitHub Packages as <code class="green">@adhil72/gitvault</code>. Point the scope at GitHub's registry and sign in with a token that has <code>read:packages</code>:</p>
      <pre class="code"><span class="c"># .npmrc</span>
@adhil72:registry=https://npm.pkg.github.com
//npm.pkg.github.com/:_authToken=&#36;&#123;NODE_AUTH_TOKEN&#125;

<span class="g">$</span> npm install @adhil72/gitvault</pre>
      <p>Load one environment before anything else runs:</p>
      <pre class="code"><span class="g">import '@adhil72/gitvault/config'</span> <span class="c">// uses GITVAULT_ENV, then NODE_ENV</span></pre>
      <p>Or call it yourself. It is synchronous, returns only the names it set (never values), and keeps variables that are already set unless you pass <code>override</code>:</p>
      <pre class="code"><span class="g">import</span> {'{'} config, GitVaultError {'}'} <span class="g">from</span> '@adhil72/gitvault'

<span class="g">try</span> {'{'}
  <span class="g">const</span> {'{'} environment, loaded {'}'} = config('production')
{'}'} <span class="g">catch</span> (e) {'{'}
  <span class="c">// e.code: E_LOCKED, E_PERMISSION_DENIED, E_NOT_AUTHENTICATED, E_NOT_INSTALLED ...</span>
{'}'}</pre>
      <p>The library asks the installed <code>gitvault</code> CLI, so the CLI must be on PATH (or set <code>GITVAULT_BIN</code>). Secrets are never logged or written to disk.</p>

      <h3 id="other" class="sub">Other languages, scripts and tools</h3>
      <p>Use the CLI directly. Secrets are injected for that one process and never printed:</p>
      <pre class="code"><span class="g">$</span> gitvault run --environment production -- python app.py
<span class="g">$</span> gitvault run --environment development -- docker compose up
<span class="g">$</span> gitvault --json env export production <span class="c"># machine-readable, for tooling</span></pre>
      <p>AI tools can use secrets without seeing the values through the MCP server: <code>gitvault mcp</code>.</p>

      <h3 id="servers" class="sub">Servers and CI</h3>
      <p>Nothing interactive is needed. Set <code class="green">GITVAULT_TOKEN</code> (a read-only GitHub token for the vault repo) and <code class="green">GITVAULT_DEVICE_KEY</code> (from <code>gitvault device trust --name prod-server --export</code>). Both are removed from the process after use, and you can revoke a key any time with <code>gitvault device revoke</code>. <code>GITVAULT_PASSWORD</code> also works. A locked vault never prompts: it fails with <code>E_LOCKED</code>.</p>
      <p>To limit a developer machine to one project, run <code>gitvault trust add . --env development</code> inside it. The device then unlocks by itself only in that folder.</p>

      <div class="next">
        <a href={href('/security/')}><b>Security model →</b><span>How your secrets are encrypted and stored.</span></a>
        <a href={href('/changelog/')}><b>Changelog →</b><span>What changed in each release.</span></a>
      </div>
    </article>
    <aside class="toc"><h4>ON THIS PAGE</h4>{sections.map(([id, t]) => <a href={`#${id}`}>{t}</a>)}</aside>
  </div>
</Base>
